WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
But first up, he is the mayor
of New Orleans, his new book

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.802
is<i> In the Shadow of Statues:
 a White Southerner
 Confronts History,</i>

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:03.936
Mitch Landrieu!

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:06.239
-(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)
-Hey!

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.942
-How are you, Mr. Mayor?
-I'm doing great, how are you?

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:13.046
Good to see you at home!
We're happy to have you.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:14.013
As am I.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.250
-Welcome to sunny Los Angeles
-It's great to be here.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:18.717
I hear you're running
for president.

00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:20.219
-I am not.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:22.221
See, I thought I'd just get
right in, and...

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.023
-I thought I was
gonna trick you.
-That was very clever.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.992
-Very clever.
You're not running, or...?
-I am not.

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:29.261
-Shermanesque, or...?
Not running at all?
-(LAUGHS) I'm not running.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.163
-How about that?
-Because okay,
kinda looks like it! Okay.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.234
-(LAUGHS)
-Well, because, it's about time
we had a mayor...

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:35.902
-Have we ever had a mayor--
-Never, never.

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:37.003
-Never!
-Never.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.139
But mayors are the ones
who are taking care of stuff
in this country, right?

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.041
They're the ones who actually
have to respond,

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.177
because, first of all,
people live in cities.

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:46.546
That's right. 85 percent
of people in America
live in cities.

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:49.482
-Is that right? 85 percent. Wow.
-Yeah. Yeah.
Isn't that something?

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.584
Actually, mayors...
You know, one of the things

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.686
that all of the mayors
of America are doing,

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.656
non-ideologically-based,
they're practical people,
they get things done.

00:00:56.722 --> 00:00:59.091
-Right. You have to.
-And that's because they seek--
Well, this is why.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.527
Because they're
in the carpool line.
They're in the grocery store.

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:03.796
-Right.
-They're at the cleaners.
And if you do something wrong,

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:07.199
somebody pops you, and if you
make a decision, it hits
the ground the next day.

00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:11.170
And the most innovative things
in the country are happening
in cities, right now.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.172
And it's just very exciting.
It's a good time to be there,

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:15.541
and we've got a great crop
of mayors across America
as we speak.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.277
Yeah. Our one here wants
to be president.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.112
-He sure does. He's a good guy.
-Yeah. Oh.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.145
I think he's got a shot.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:22.081
-So, you're supporting him?
-I may be.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.950
-(LAUGHS)
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.252
-And then, maybe not.
-I'm gonna keep trying,
you know?

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.588
You also have a great senator
who's running.
Kamala Harris is great, too.

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:32.192
Yeah, we got-- I think
the Democrats have an actual
good bench.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.927
I think there will be about 150
people who decide...

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.963
-Yeah.
-...to try to do
something different.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.631
Yes, because--
Well, because a lot of them

00:01:38.697 --> 00:01:41.000
perceive the current president
as something of a buffoon.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.235
-Well, I think they're right.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.803
-So... Yeah.
-I think they're right.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.806
I think the country feels...
I mean, right now, obviously,

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.976
the president thinks that
governing in chaos
is a great strategy.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.978
-Right.
-I don't think the rest
of America thinks

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:53.646
-that it's a great strategy.
-Right.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:56.382
-And it's interesting, because--
-(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:01:56.449 --> 00:02:00.553
-I mean, you say 85 percent
of people live in cities...
-Yeah. And they will be.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.622
...and we are, of course,
a very divided country,

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.359
and one of the ways
we're divided is city mouse
versus country mouse,

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:11.163
and it does seem like
Donald Trump is never
on the side of the city mouse.

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:13.165
-Well, I think that's--
-He's always
with the country people.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:15.601
-That's one way to look at it.
-Which is weird,
because he's from New York.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.370
(LAUGHS) Exactly. That's one way
to look at it, but the truth is,

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:19.939
for people, even,
that live in cities,

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:22.475 line:0%
a lot of people that don't
live in cities work in them
every day,

00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:24.176 line:0%
and a lot of people
in the cities depend

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.178 line:0%
on what people
in the rural areas produce,

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:28.748 line:0%
and the people
in the rural areas
depend on jobs in the city.

00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:31.985
So, on the ground every day,
if people don't notice this,

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:34.820
that we're actually amongst
each other, living in peace
and harmony,

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:36.322
-and actually doing a good job.
-Right.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.724
When we talk about
President Trump, we are divided.

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:42.662
Yeah. What-- Now, he's going
after sanctuary cities.

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:44.630
-Correct.
-What is New Orleans'
status, there?

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.866
-What is your position
on sanctuary?
-Well, interestingly enough,

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.570
"sanctuary cities" was a term
coined by the church,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.171
don't jump out of your seat...

00:02:52.237 --> 00:02:54.473
-(BILL LAUGHS)
-...you know, to give...
Because I understand,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.908
-we can talk about that later.
-Okay.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:03:00.613
But-- To give comfort to those
that were in need of aid
and afflicted.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.949
President Trump has now
turned it into a weapon...

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:07.220
that follows his theory that
all Mexicans are rapists,

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:10.890
is that when cities are open
and welcoming places, somehow,
they're harboring criminals,

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:12.991
which could not be anything
further from the truth.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.394
So, mayors across America,
and police chiefs,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.930
have tried to communicate
to the attorney general,
to the president,

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.498
that actually,
they have it backwards.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:22.402
You see, when a police officer
shows up
at a rape victim's house,

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:25.338
and the first thing
they ask them is not,
"How are you?"

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:27.406
but, like, "What is your
immigration status?"

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:30.276
generally, what happens is
the rapist goes free.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.945
And so, what mayors
across America are saying,

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.848
which is that,
"Our number-one priority is
to protect the people,

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.317
irrespective of their
immigration status."

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:42.121
And so, what we need to do
is we have to fight criminals
where they are,

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:45.225
and not get confused
that because people are not
from here,

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:48.260
they might be more prone
to crime than anywhere else.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.730
-And, of course--
-(APPLAUSE)

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:56.235
It goes... It follows right into
the entire gun violence issue
that we're having in America,

00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:58.170
which is catastrophic
for us all,

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:00.873
and mayors in America are
saying, "We know how to protect
the streets of America.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.008
We need police officers
that are properly trained,

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.277
we need police officers that
understand community policing,

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:07.513
-we need--" I'm sorry?
-Are they? Because...

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.949
Are police officers
properly trained? Because we had
another shooting--

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:11.784
No, I said, "We need
police officers who are
properly trained."

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.552
-I know, and I'm asking
if they are.
-Right. Well--

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.854
-Because we saw
in Sacramento, here--
-Just the other day.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:17.656
-Yeah. Another--
-That was a terrible episode.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:20.092
-A terrible episode that we've
seen many times before.
-Correct.

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:23.495
And I just want to know
what you think about...

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:27.266
<i>Is the approach of the police
 correct to begin with?</i>

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:31.937
Because it seems like this
emptying the whole clip,
20 shots, into somebody...

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:35.674
-Right.
-...who you're not sure of
what they did, what they have,

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:36.842
-is crazy.
-Right.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:40.246
And I've never heard of...
I'm not saying every policeman
would do that,

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:44.684
but I've never heard a policeman
say anything but, "I defend
my fellow officers doing that."

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.188
-Well, let me speak to that--
-And it seems to me, at best,
this is a horrible way to train

00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:50.723
police to do their job.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.326
Let me put it in context
for you, because the city
of New Orleans, my city,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.095 line:0%
on the first day that I came
in to office,

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:57.896 line:0%
invited the Justice Department
to come in,

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.799 line:0%
and we are operating under
one of the most intense
consent decrees

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:04.536 line:0%
to hire the right people,
supervise in the right way,
train them the correct way,

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.840
and actually begin to work
with them on appropriate
use of force.

00:05:07.973 --> 00:05:09.608
Everybody knows,
we don't have to say this,

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.778
most police officers are
really good folks that try
to do the right thing.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.981
There are police officers
that have been poorly hired
and poorly trained,

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:17.283
and we're retraining our
police departments as we speak.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:19.352
So, for example,
in New Orleans now,

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:22.055
every one of our police officers
has a body camera on them.

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:23.889
When there is
a police-involved shooting,

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.058
as soon as that happens,
the scene gets shut down,

00:05:26.124 --> 00:05:29.562
we have what's called
an independent police monitor
that goes out to the scene,

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.565
we have a team of investigators
with the FBI embedded,

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:37.937
we have, now, the practice
of releasing the tapes early.
So now, everything's on tape,

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:40.906
-and if it is a shoot that is
in excessive force...
-But--

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:43.843
-...the police officer is
disciplined and goes to jail.
-But I guess I'm asking a more

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:47.145
basic question. It seems like
the police have this idea

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:50.249
in their head that it should be
a completely no-risk operation,

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:52.918
-and we know it is
a job with risk.
-Right.

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:55.521
-By the way, they do keep
statistics on this stuff.
-Correct.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:59.258
A policeman is the 14th-ranked
risky job in America.

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:01.093
-Correct. Correct.
-It's not the most risky job.

00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:04.964
-Well, we have had episodes--
-But they seem to have this idea
that, "If I feel my life is

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.434
threatened in any way,
I have the right to just
take you out."

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.036
-Correct. That is--
-This guy was a thief.

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:11.670
I don't think he deserved
to be executed.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:14.306
Most police chiefs
in America will tell you that
that is wrong,

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:16.642
and that now, we're training
police officers not to do that,

00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:18.644
to be discerning,
to be very thoughtful.

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:21.981
We have had a lot of episodes
where police officers have been
killed, as well.

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.383
And so, it's a really
tough balance,
but in New Orleans...

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.952
I think you're doing it
in Los Angeles.

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:28.887
There are a number of other
cities that I can't remember
that are under consent decree,

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:31.023
where we're retraining
our officers to make sure

00:06:31.089 --> 00:06:33.959
that they understand exactly
when force is necessary
and when it's not,

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:35.862
and now there are consequences
to that.

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:37.796
And so, they're getting better.
We have a long way to go.

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:40.432
-Okay.
-The issue in Sacramento
will play itself out.

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:43.535
We will investigate that,
find out what
the circumstances are,

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:45.671
and hopefully, they'll make
the right determination
about it.

00:06:45.737 --> 00:06:47.773
All right. So, let's talk
about guns, now.

00:06:47.839 --> 00:06:49.141
-Yeah.
-Because that's certainly--

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:52.177
I mean, when you're a policeman
in this country... (LAUGHS)

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:54.313
-...you have to figure that
into the equation.
-You have to be worried

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.349
-about it every day.
-Right. And tomorrow is
the big march...

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:59.651
-Yeah.
-...that came out
of the Parkland shooting.

00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:02.187
Were you... Now, I've been
critical of Democrats

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.956
for always starting
the argument with,

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:07.326
-"Well, I'm a big supporter
of the Second Amendment..."
-Well, I'll end it there.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.562
-You are?
-No, no, let me...

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:11.363
-Okay.
-Let me articulate it for you
first...

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.199
-Yeah, please.
-...because I'm from the South.

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.934
All right. First of all,
I'm a lawyer, and I believe
in the Constitution.

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:17.369
Written throughout
the Constitution

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:19.872
is a tension between giving
people's rights,

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:23.843
but we always forget that
there's always a responsibility
that attaches to that.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.512
And you can be
for the Second Amendment,

00:07:26.578 --> 00:07:29.515
in other words, you can
acknowledge an individual's
right to own a gun

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.416
to hunt
and to protect themselves,

00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:34.921
but you can also be in favor
of thoughtful, responsible
gun ownership.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:38.124
I'll give you a phrase
that I think everybody
in America's gotta agree with.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.726
-Right.
-Not every American needs
any kind of gun

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.628
at any time
to do whatever they want.

00:07:42.761 --> 00:07:44.463
-And so, as a consequence...
-Right. (LAUGHS) Yeah.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.999
-Right? I mean, it's not hard!
-It's a good way to put it.
Yeah, no, I mean...

00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:50.236
-(APPLAUSE)
-So, as a consequence,
it's not unreasonable--

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:51.837
-You could run on that.
-Yes, I could.

00:07:51.903 --> 00:07:53.839
-If you were running,
which you're, of course, not.
-If I was, but I'm not.

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.474
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.677
So, there's--
It is not unreasonable,

00:07:57.743 --> 00:08:00.446 line:0%
and it's not
an unreasonable restriction on
a Second Amendment right,

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:02.581 line:0%
to have universal
background checks,

00:08:02.681 --> 00:08:05.717 line:0%
or to outlaw weapons of war,
or to have waiting periods,

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:08.019 line:0%
or to outlaw bump stocks.
That's not unreasonable.

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.555 line:0%
As a matter of fact,
the NRA used to
kinda hang around that area.

00:08:10.622 --> 00:08:11.990
Now they've gone
to the extreme.

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:14.960
But let me just give you
a number. This will shock you,
but it's true, you can test it.

00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:21.834
Since 1980, less than 38 years
in this country,
630,000 American citizens

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.103
have been killed
on the streets of America.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.571
-On the streets?
-On the streets of America.
All right?

00:08:26.638 --> 00:08:29.474
-That doesn't include suicide?
-Well, no, it does, as well,
but that's in homes.

00:08:29.541 --> 00:08:31.110
-Okay.
-So, with gun violence.

00:08:31.377 --> 00:08:36.548
That's more people than soldiers
that have been killed in all
the wars of the 20th Century.

00:08:36.615 --> 00:08:40.318
So, when people say something
really simple that they want
you to accept,

00:08:40.619 --> 00:08:44.056
like, "Guns don't kill people,"
you just have to stop there

00:08:44.123 --> 00:08:46.092
and say,
"Well, no, actually, they do."

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.527
They were created to kill,
and sometimes they kill animals,

00:08:48.593 --> 00:08:50.095
-sometimes they kill people.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:52.197
-Right.
-And then you can say,

00:08:52.264 --> 00:08:54.233
"Well, if we can't agree
on that, we can agree on this,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.801
that people actually use guns
to kill people. That's true.

00:08:57.068 --> 00:08:58.570
But you can't say that guns
don't kill."

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:01.439
And I think reasonable
gun restrictions make
a lot of sense,

00:09:01.506 --> 00:09:04.810
and I think, if you poll it,
if Congress really cared
about this,

00:09:04.976 --> 00:09:08.947
most of the people in America
believe in people having
a right to own a gun,

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:10.850
but demand responsible
gun ownership,

00:09:10.916 --> 00:09:12.551
and it seems to me that we can
get there.

00:09:12.618 --> 00:09:15.287
And these kids are gonna
demonstrate to us, once again,

00:09:15.353 --> 00:09:18.357
what we adults ought to be
thinking about
as we go forward.

00:09:18.424 --> 00:09:21.761
-(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)
-Okay, last question,
and then I'll let you go.

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:23.529
Uh... (CHUCKLES)

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:27.499
Your book is about taking down
the statues,

00:09:27.566 --> 00:09:29.234
the Confederate statues
in your city.

00:09:29.301 --> 00:09:32.204
-I know you took a lot of heat
from certain factions.
-I did. I did.

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:35.908
Um... What has been
the upshot, and if...

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:39.445
-Say a mayor did that,
and was running for president.
-Let's just say.

00:09:39.611 --> 00:09:41.113
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)
-Or not.

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:43.115
-How-- No, not you!
-No, not me!

00:09:43.181 --> 00:09:44.683
-I'm saying a hypothetical.
-Yeah.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:48.354
-Asking for a friend.
-Of course. Just between
me and you. (LAUGHS)

00:09:48.487 --> 00:09:49.588
How would... (LAUGHS)

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:52.224
-How would that issue,
do you think, play out?
-Well, the truth--

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:56.162
-Considering what you did?
-The book is... It uses
the prism of the statues,

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:58.263
-which is really about race
in America.
-Right.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.833
And it's pretty simple.
We need to stop judging people

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.203
based on their race, creed,
color, religion,
nation of origin,

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:07.973
and do what Americans always do,
or what we're supposed to do,
aspirationally,

00:10:08.039 --> 00:10:09.875
is judge people based
on their behavior.

00:10:09.975 --> 00:10:12.545
See, that's a really
simple idea,
and we haven't done that.

00:10:12.611 --> 00:10:15.481
And the book also talks
about the speech that I gave

00:10:15.547 --> 00:10:17.316
that's called "The Truth,"
which is like,

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:21.687
"Quit saying that the Civil War
was not about slavery,"

00:10:21.754 --> 00:10:24.756
or, "Quit saying that
the Civil War was some kind
of noble cause."

00:10:24.823 --> 00:10:25.891
You see, it wasn't.

00:10:26.191 --> 00:10:28.227
The Civil War was fought
to destroy the United States
of America,

00:10:28.293 --> 00:10:29.528
not to put it back together.

00:10:29.594 --> 00:10:31.429
It was fought over the cause
of slavery.

00:10:31.496 --> 00:10:32.731
-Right.
-And for some reason,

00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:34.333
we have a difficult time
dealing with that issue.

00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:37.136
So, when somebody says
something like,

00:10:37.203 --> 00:10:39.170
"I wanna make America
great, again,"

00:10:39.504 --> 00:10:42.641
it's the "comma, again,"
that causes a problem...

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:45.044
-Right.
-...for people. And if you
listen to African Americans,

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.546
sixty percent of which
run the city of New Orleans,

00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:49.881
they say, "That 'again' thing
really bothers me."

00:10:49.948 --> 00:10:51.182
You see, that's a dog whistle...

00:10:51.249 --> 00:10:52.618
-Right.
-...about where we wanna
go back to.

00:10:52.685 --> 00:10:54.086
-Right.
-When do we wanna go back to?

00:10:54.153 --> 00:10:56.889
-Where were you back in 1950,
and how was it for you?
-Right. Yeah.

00:10:56.956 --> 00:10:58.424
-It wasn't great for everybody.
-Right.

00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:02.194
And so, that notion
of racial reconciliation
is critically important,

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.797
but essentially, it spans across
the issue of immigration,

00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:07.800
guns and violence... I mean,
everything that you talk about.

00:11:07.866 --> 00:11:09.501
Who lives in cities
and who doesn't.

00:11:09.568 --> 00:11:11.270
"Let's not go
to the urban center."

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:13.638
-Can you hear it?
-Yes.

00:11:13.772 --> 00:11:16.709
-Okay, good, you heard
that dog whistle.
-We've heard it for years.

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:19.177
And so, the issue was
to confront the issue
of race in America,

00:11:19.244 --> 00:11:21.980
because we are a nation
that believes that everybody
ought to have a shot.

00:11:22.047 --> 00:11:23.182
All right.

00:11:23.282 --> 00:11:25.150
-I think you ought
to have a shot!
-(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.287
-You ought to have a shot.
-I think you'd make
a fantastic non-candidate,

00:11:28.353 --> 00:11:29.855
-but you do what you wanna do.
-(LAUGHS)

00:11:29.921 --> 00:11:31.390
Mayor Mitch Landrieu.
Thank you, Mitch.

